   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, cloud/r1181-opus, round 1): REQUEST CHANGES** — head 9705210\n\n1. **Medium**, `App/SwiftHFR/Settings/InfoBubbleButton.swift:19`. The branch tries to predict the system's adaptation from the horizontal size class alone, but now that `.popover` is no longer forced, the system adapts it on **both** axes (SwiftUI has `presentationCompactAdaptation(horizontal:vertical:)` for this reason). Failing scenario: on a Plus or Pro Max iPhone in landscape (regular width, compact height), Réglages sits in the Plus tab's stack. `TabSplitWidth` is not applied there (`RootTabsView.swift:95/116` and `MessagesRootView.swift:22` only; `RootNavigationView.swift:210-213` says \"the Plus tab … keep the regular width\"). So `sizeClass == .regular` and the code takes the `else` branch: a bare `bubble()` with no `ScrollView` and no detents. If the system then shows a sheet there (compact height), that sheet holds unscrollable `fixedSize` text on a very short screen. That is the clipping #1181 fixes, in its worst geometry. Fix: stop depending on the prediction. Either (a) set `.presentationCompactAdaptation(horizontal: .sheet, vertical: .sheet)` and decide from both size classes, or (b) make the content fit both forms whatever the system picks: `ViewThatFits(in: .vertical) { bubble(); ScrollView { bubble() } }` with the detents always set (a popover ignores them). Add a test for the regular-width, compact-height case.\n2. **Low**, `App/SwiftHFRTests/Settings/InfoBubblePresentationTests.swift:7-15`. The tests restate the enum's constants. They \"fail without the change\" only because the code does not compile, and nothing ties `InfoBubbleButton` to `isSheet` or `sheetDetents`. Once 1 is fixed, cover the two-axis rule (`isSheet(horizontal:vertical:)`) so the Pro Max landscape case is pinned.\n3. **Detail**, `InfoBubbleButton.swift:20`. In the sheet, the content is not stretched to full width, so a short `VStack(alignment: .leading)` bubble (the default-browser and minimum-quotes rows) shows as a centred block. Add `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)` inside the `ScrollView`.\n\nNote: the PR body asks for the operator to confirm the change from the 2026-11-17 bubble decision (#1062, #1065). The operator's triage on #1181 lists this option, but get that confirmation before merging.\n\n**Verified**: diff `origin/dev-swift...9705210`, issue #1181 and its comments, PR #1341 body, all four call sites, the size-class overrides in `RootNavigationView` and `RootTabsView`, and `PlusView`. `swiftlint lint --strict` on both changed files passes (rc=0). By reading, the code compiles. No Xcode build: the tests were not run and the popover's adaptation in landscape was not measured.\n\nRound 1 fixed in 9539934 with option (b): no prediction any more. The popover content is `ViewThatFits(in: .vertical) { bubble(); ScrollView { bubble().frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading) } }` with the detents always set (finding 1 and 3), so the content fits whichever form the system picks on either axis. Finding 2: the two-axis rule no longer exists (nothing is predicted), so there is no rule left to test beyond the detents; the test says what only the Mac check can prove (sheet scrolls at AX sizes; popover stays on iPad and on Plus/Pro Max landscape). Round 2 requested.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
