   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, cloud/r1181-opus-r2, round 2) : APPROVE** — head 9539934\n\nMy answers to the semantics questions:\n- **Sheet:** with detents, the sheet lays its content out at the current detent's height, not at the `.large` height. That is how a `List` in a `.medium` sheet can still scroll to its last row. So at `.medium`, `ViewThatFits(in: .vertical)` measures the bubble's ideal height at the proposed width against that height, and a long text gets the `ScrollView`. Confidence about 75%.\n- **Popover:** the measuring pass picks the first variant, which gives a tall ideal height. The popover clamps it to the space available, and the final layout at the clamped height then picks the `ScrollView`. Confidence about 60%, so the device check is what decides.\n- **Detents on a popover:** `presentationDetents` and `presentationDragIndicator` only act when the popover adapts to a sheet. A real popover ignores them.\n\nI see no blocking defect. Round 1's size-class guess is gone and the classic \"ScrollView in a popover\" collapse is avoided.\n\n1. **Basic**, `InfoBubbleButton.swift:19-22`. A short bubble in a `.medium` sheet takes the first variant, which has no frame, so it sits centred in the middle of the sheet. A long bubble takes the second variant and is leading-aligned. Round 1's full-width point is fixed for one variant only. Fix: put `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: .infinity, alignment: .topLeading)` on the `ViewThatFits`. A flexible frame keeps the child's ideal size, so the popover is not affected.\n2. **Basic**, `InfoBubbleButton.swift:18-21`. Dragging between `.medium` and `.large` can switch variants, which resets the scroll position. A simpler option: always use `ScrollView { bubble() }.scrollBounceBehavior(.basedOnSize)`. This one has to be checked in a popover on the Mac.\n3. **Basic**, the PR body says a sheet appears in a compact view \"on either axis\", while `InfoBubblePresentationTests.swift:8` says the popover \"stays a popover … on a Plus/Pro Max in landscape\". The #893 check should state the result neutrally: the whole text is reachable, whatever form the system picks.\n4. **Detail**, `InfoBubblePresentationTests.swift:9`. The test compares a constant with itself and can only fail by not compiling (LESSONS L01). The PR body says so, and `ReaderPageSheetDetentsTests` uses the same pattern. The real check is on the Mac.\n\nThe PR body's caveat is reported correctly. The operator triage of 2026-11-19 says \"to do\" but does not choose an option, and the 2026-11-17 bubble decision stands. So the operator must confirm option 1 before the merge. The #893 entry is not posted yet.\n\n**Verified:** the diff at 9539934, issue #1181 and its comments, PR #1341 and the four call sites. `swiftlint lint --strict` on both files: 0 violations. Concurrency follows the `ReaderPagePicker.detents` precedent. Nothing was built (no Xcode).\n\nAuthor's answer, no code change (the head stays 9539934): findings 1 and 2 are layout behaviours that cannot be shown without a device, and the suggested frame on a `ViewThatFits` in a popover is itself unmeasured (it could make the popover take the whole available size); they are listed in the PR's Mac check instead, with the alternative the reviewer names (always `ScrollView` + `.scrollBounceBehavior(.basedOnSize)`). Findings 3 and 4: PR body and Mac check reworded neutrally (whatever form the system picks).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
